  "detail": "Thank you: the classification is structural and the fallback paths are right. One defect in the new worker path.\n\nWhen a worker reports an authentication failure, lanAgentExecutor adopts the report (line ~201) and returns `fallback(id)` (line ~217). The completed job stays attached to the run's state (`agentJob`). If that local session then edits files and ends in quota exhaustion, or another authentication failure, `Context.invoke` moves to the next candidate. That candidate reopens the same completed job and tries to adopt it again. The clean-worktree guard in src/pipeline/engine.ts (~line 1268) then stops the run with `Coordinator HEAD or worktree changed during worker session`, so the next provider never runs. Before this PR, a worker authentication error was returned as the round's result, so the intermediate local session didn't exist.\n\nReview reproduced this in test/pipeline-routing.test.ts. Configure workers for implement. Make enqueueAgentJob queue the job, mark it leased and complete it with `{status: \"unavailable\", failureClass: \"authentication\", sha: request.parentSha, ...}`. The first provider's local session writes partial.txt and returns status quota; the second succeeds. Expected: the run succeeds and the implementers are [first, second]. Actual: needs_human with the message above.\n\nFix: before handing execution to the local session, retire that worker job durably, keeping its publication state, so that no later candidate in the same invocation reopens it. Apply the same retirement to the existing confinement-startup fallback, which uses the same `fallback(id)` path. Add the regression test above; it must fail without the fix."
